metaDescription: "How tree removal works: arborist assessment, rigging, stump grinding, storm cleanup and what drives tree removal cost. Call 24/7 for same day help."
svcFaqs:
  - q: "Can a tree be removed without damaging the lawn?"
    a: "Mostly, yes. Crews lay plywood or ground mats under heavy equipment, lower wood on ropes instead of dropping it, and keep trucks on the driveway where they can reach. Expect some shallow tire marks or flattened grass, which recover within a few weeks."
  - q: "What happens to the wood after removal?"
    a: "By default, branches are chipped and hauled and the trunk is cut and taken away. You can ask to keep firewood rounds cut to length, or a load of wood chips for mulch. Keeping wood on site often lowers the price because there is less to haul."
  - q: "How does a crew remove a tree that is too close to the house to fell?"
    a: "It is dismantled from the top down. A climber or bucket truck removes limbs, then the trunk is cut into short sections that are lowered on ropes through a friction device at the base. In tight spaces a crane can lift whole sections over the house."
  - q: "Can a certified arborist tell me if a tree really needs to come down?"
    a: "Yes. An arborist looks at lean, root plate movement, cavities, fungal growth at the base, cracks and dead sections of the crown. Some trees can be kept with pruning or cabling; others are already a hazard. You get a straight answer either way."
  - q: "How long does a typical tree removal take?"
    a: "A small or medium tree in open ground is often finished in one to three hours including cleanup. A large tree over a house, or several trees, can take a full day. Crane jobs and storm work depend on access and how much damage is involved."
  - q: "How deep does stump grinding go?"
    a: "Usually 6 to 12 inches below the soil surface, which is deep enough to lay sod or plant shrubs. Deeper grinding is possible for a new tree in the same spot or for construction, and should be requested in advance."
---
