<section class="sec sec--alt" aria-labelledby="causes-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="causes-title">Why trees end up needing removal</h2>
      <p class="answer">Almost always one of four things: the tree is dead or dying, it is structurally failing, it was damaged in a storm, or it is simply in the wrong place.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p><strong>Disease and pests.</strong> Emerald ash borer has killed ash trees across much of the eastern half of the country. Oak wilt, Dutch elm disease, pine beetles and root rot fungi do the same to other species. Once the canopy is more than about half dead, removal is usually the only option.</p>
      <p><strong>Structural defects.</strong> Co-dominant trunks with a tight V-shaped union, deep cavities, cracks that open and close in the wind, and roots cut by past construction all weaken a tree long before it falls.</p>
      <p><strong>Storm damage.</strong> Wind, ice and saturated soil snap trunks, split crowns and lift root plates. A tree that loses a large share of its crown often declines over the next few seasons even if it survives the storm.</p>
      <p><strong>Location.</strong> Trees planted too close to the foundation, septic field, pool or driveway outgrow the space. Roots lift slabs and limbs grind against roofs.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec" aria-labelledby="diag-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="diag-title">How an arborist decides whether a tree should come down</h2>
      <p class="answer">By looking for specific defects, not by guessing from size or age.</p>
      <p>The assessment starts at the ground and works up. Soil heaving or cracking on one side of the trunk means the root plate is moving. Mushrooms or conks at the base point to internal decay. A sounding mallet and, if needed, a resistance drill show how much solid wood is left inside the trunk.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Higher up, the arborist checks for deadwood, cracks, cavities, weak branch unions and how the crown weight is distributed over the lean. They also look at the target: what the tree would hit if it failed. A defective tree over an empty field and the same tree over a bedroom are very different risks.</p>
      <p>The outcome is one of three recommendations: leave it alone, prune or cable it to reduce the risk, or remove it. If removal is the answer, the same walk-around produces the rigging plan, the equipment list and the price.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec sec--wood" aria-labelledby="options-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2 id="options-title">Ways to take a tree down</h2>
      <p>No single method is best. The right one depends on space, the condition of the wood and what is underneath.</p>
    </div>
    <ul class="usps">
      <li><h3>Straight felling</h3><p>Fastest and cheapest. Needs a clear landing zone at least as long as the tree is tall.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Sectional dismantling</h3><p>Climber or bucket truck removes the tree in pieces lowered on ropes. The standard method near houses.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Crane removal</h3><p>Sections are cut and lifted out. Used over roofs, pools, or when dead wood is unsafe to climb.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Pruning or cabling</h3><p>Sometimes the tree can stay. Removing weight or adding support cables can fix the problem.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

