<section class="sec sec--alt" aria-labelledby="cost-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col two-col--wide">
    <div>
      <h2 id="cost-title">What affects tree removal cost</h2>
      <p class="answer">Size is only part of it. Risk and access usually matter more.</p>
      <p>Two trees of the same height can differ by a thousand dollars. The one standing in an open backyard is felled in an hour. The one leaning over the roof, behind a fence with no truck access, needs a climber, rigging and twice the crew time.</p>
      <p>You always get the price before work starts, and it covers the cleanup that is included in the job.</p>
    </div>
    <ul class="why-list">
      <li><strong>Height and trunk diameter.</strong> More wood means more cutting and hauling.</li>
      <li><strong>Proximity to structures and lines.</strong> Anything that rules out felling adds rigging time.</li>
      <li><strong>Access.</strong> Whether a bucket truck, crane or chipper can reach the tree.</li>
      <li><strong>Condition of the wood.</strong> Dead or rotten trees are slower and riskier to climb.</li>
      <li><strong>Emergency timing.</strong> Night, weekend and storm callouts take crews off scheduled work.</li>
      <li><strong>Extras.</strong> Stump grinding, firewood cutting or full haul-away of the trunk.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

